00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  have you ever sat down to take a test  and completely forgotten what you had  studied or  opened your fridge and not remembered  what you came to get  quite often we only remember these  things once we returned to the place  where we studied  or to where we had the initial craving  for that snack  these experiences are so common that  psychologists have studied them for  years  and attribute them to something called  context dependent memory  what is context dependent memory and how  does it work  to understand let's first have a look at  how memories are formed  this usually happens in three stages  encoding storage and retrieval  when you learn something new or have a  new experience  say you meet someone at a party your  brain takes this information  the person's face the sound of their  voice  and transforms it into a memory code a  pattern of activity  in a network of nerve cells this is  known as encoding  the encoded memories are temporarily  stored in short-term memory  and if you call upon them enough they  can get transferred to a more permanent  storage  called long-term memory from where they  can be recalled whenever you need to use  them  this process of accessing information  from long-term memory  is known as retrieval the interesting  thing about forming memories  is that your brain encodes and stores  not only the information that you're  actively focused on  but also some of the contextual  information from your surroundings  things you might overlook like the music  playing in the background  a subtle aroma in the room or the  physical layout of the space  are encoded in addition to your new  friend's face and  name this link between the context and  the thing you want to remember  is the basis for context dependent  memory  when you find yourself struggling to  remember the name of your dance partner  listening to the song that you dance to  or revisiting the location of the party  might help you remember if the context  during retrieval  is similar to the context during  encoding you are  much more likely to recall the memory  the idea of context-dependent memory is  not  only intuitive it has also been  scientifically supported with  experimental data  in one of the classic studies on context  dependent memory  researchers asked two groups of divers  to memorize a list of words in two  different contexts  one group memorized the words while they  were under water  and the other group did this on land  after the words had been memorized  the researchers asked half the divers to  recall the words  in their original learning context and  the other half  to recall the words in the context where  they did not learn the words  the researchers found that words learned  underwater were best recalled underwater  and vice versa other studies have also  shown that internal states and  moods such as feelings of hunger  happiness  or being caffeinated at the time of  memory encoding  can help in the contextualization of  memory  and operate as the retrieval cue  so what affects the strength of a  context-dependent memory  some of the factors that enhance  context-dependent memory at the time of  encoding  are the richness of the context if a  high number of cues  stimulate many different senses the  memory will be better contextualized  the uniqueness of the context save the  high volume of the music playing and  the level of attention you paid the  small details in your surroundings  among factors that interfere with  forming a strong context dependent  memory  are high stress levels or fatigue at the  time of encoding  since these things decrease your  attentiveness to contextual details  now that you know how a specific smell  sound  or feeling can influence the way you  form and recall memories  you can try to use context dependent  memory to your advantage  the next time you're preparing for a  test try studying in a similar  environment to the one you will take  your test in  and if you forget what you wanted from  the fridge try revisiting the place  where you first thought about what snack  you wanted most likely you'll find the  answers that you knew  all along  [Music]  you

